[{"body":"We are trying Immutables, and definitely want to use it. It's working well in both Gradle and Eclipse, but we have one issue which manifests as a NPE on a project clean inside eclipse (and have no issues in gradle). The stack trace is included at the bottom of this issue.

We have identified a possible cause as when we have two specification interfaces, one of which refers to the generated class of the other. We're not sure whether this should be possible or not (it's certainly useful to us, but as new users we don't know it it's an anti-pattern).

Here's an example
```
@Immutable
public interface Container
{
    public ImmutableContents getContents();
}

@Immutable
public interface Contents
{
    public Integer getSomeContentSpecificThing();
}
```
It appears that you may need a couple of other classes in the same package to coerce the exception during a clean-and-build, and given the NPE below, it appears that the processor has trouble working out which order to process things.

In the interim, we're just using the specifying interfaces directly, but then needing to use the from() methods to get access to the fluent withX methods seems a little less parsimonious than the rest of the library, which seems excellent thus far.

We'd love to get a read on this from people more experienced with the library.

```
